<interrogationes unitas="organum">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I load and validate a widget manifest from its text?
    <responsum symbolum="organum_legere organum_legere_genus">organum_legere(fons,
      piscina, intern, &amp;vitia) parses the STML, judges it against
      the embedded organum canon, then applies the loader rules. It
      returns NIHIL if there is ANY vitium, and *vitia is always set to
      a Xar of chorda messages (empty when the manifest is sound), each
      prefixed 'linea N:' (only a broken embedded canon gives an
      unprefixed message). Beware: today the loader formats several
      messages with sprintf into fixed stack buffers, so an over-long
      value (an absens of a few hundred bytes that does not fit its
      typus) overflows the stack; keep manifest values short. Apps normally do not call it directly:
      organum_legere_genus in organum_montatio reads the embedded
      manifest plus the common base and prints the vitia.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can organum_legere read the manifest file for me?
    <responsum symbolum="organum_legere organum_infixum_textus organum_legere_viam" via="lib/organa_infixa.c">No:
      the loader never touches the disk; the caller passes the text.
      Runtime manifests are embedded in lib/organa_infixa.c and come
      from organum_infixum_textus(genus, piscina); organum_legere_viam
      reads from disk for tests and tools. The organum canon itself is
      embedded too, so the loader needs no file at all.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why are some manifest errors reported by the canon and others by the loader?
    <responsum symbolum="organum_legere" via="organum.canon lib/organum.worklog.md">The
      canon judges shape (closed vocabulary, choice lists, unique
      names, scriptor citing a declared actio). Rules that involve
      several attributes at once or split a value are the loader's,
      because the canon dialect deliberately does not express them:
      sedes="motus" needs rhythmus="mobilis" and vita="transiens",
      typus="series" needs sedes="motus", orbatus needs referentia,
      referentia must be 'ligamen/genus' with a declared ligamen,
      absens must fit its typus, and optio appears only (and at least
      once) under typus="electio". The loader rules run only when the
      canon found nothing. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): these
      loader messages are sprintf'd without a bound into fixed stack
      buffers (256 bytes for the absens and unknown-ligamen messages,
      512 for the campus-title prefix), so a rule violation whose
      absens, referentia or campus nomen is a few hundred bytes long
      overflows the stack instead of being reported.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what do vita, rhythmus and sedes on a campus mean?
    <responsum symbolum="OrganumCampus organum_campus" via="organum.canon project-specs/status-genera-census.md">They
      classify one piece of widget state. vita (required): persistens
      (saved), transiens (never reaches disk) or probatio (test-only
      fields). rhythmus: discretus (discrete edits, the default) or
      mobilis (kinetic, changes continuously during a gesture). sedes:
      insula (an attribute of the widget's insula branch, the default)
      or motus (in-flight gesture state kept in memory structs next to
      Motus, never in the insula; the loader demands rhythmus mobilis
      and vita transiens for it). Only sedes="insula" fields go into
      the generated canon, starting values and owners table.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I look up one field of a loaded manifest and its default?
    <responsum symbolum="organum_campus organum_campus_index organum_numerus_camporum">organum_campus(o,
      titulus) returns the OrganumCampus or NIHIL; organum_campus_index
      and organum_numerus_camporum walk them in manifest order (base
      fields first after organum_basim_addere). All chorda members are
      NOT NUL-terminated and an absent attribute is an empty chorda
      (mensura 0, datum NIHIL); use habet_absens to tell absens=""
      from no absens at all.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I merge the common base fields into a widget manifest?
    <responsum symbolum="organum_basim_addere organum_legere_viam">organum_basim_addere(organum,
      basis) puts the base campi BEFORE the widget's own and adds the
      base actiones the widget does not already declare. It returns
      FALSUM and adds nothing if any base campus title already exists
      in the widget. Base ligamina and portae are not merged, and the
      loader rules are not run again on the combined set.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get the insula canon and starting values generated from a manifest?
    <responsum symbolum="organum_canonem_generare organum_initium organum_dominos_generare">organum_canonem_generare
      builds a canon whose root element is the organum genus, one
      attributum per sedes="insula" campus (electio options copied and
      escaped); NIHIL with *causa set if canon_legere refuses it.
      organum_initium returns the starting attributes as text, '
      titulus="absens"' for each insula campus with an absens (leading
      space, values not escaped). organum_dominos_generare builds the
      &lt;domini&gt; owners table from campi with a scriptor.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the difference between ligamina, portae and actiones in a manifest?
    <responsum symbolum="organum_ligamen_index organum_porta_index organum_actio_index">ligamina
      are entities the widget shows (props; a="parens" means the
      mounting parent supplies the value) and are what referentia
      'ligamen/genus' points at. portae are the observed outside world,
      never stored, with modus petitio or eventus. actiones are the
      names a campus scriptor may cite; systema="verum" marks one the
      system performs. Each has a numerus/index accessor pair.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
